.²8 A preposition prefixed to roots; {#nipAtAScAdayo jYeyAH prAdayastUpasargakAH .#}
 {#dyotakatvAt kriyAyoge lokAdavagatA ime .. upasargAstu vijYeyAH kriyAyogena#}
 {#viMSatiH . vivecayanti te hyarTaM nAmAKyAtaviBaktizu .. bfhaddevatA#}; {#AKyAtamupagfhyArTaviSezamime#}
 {#tasyEva sfjantItyupasargAH .#} Durga under <ls>Nirukta 1. 3</ls>. {#upetya nAmAKyAtayorarTasya#}
 {#viSezaM sfjantyutpAdayantItyupasargAH .#} <ls>Skanda P.</ls> The {#nAwyaSAstra#}
 defines {#upasarga#} thus: {#prAtipadikArTayuktaM DAtvarTamupasfjanti#}
 {#ye svArTEH . upasargA upadizwAstasmAt saMskAraSAstre'smin ..#} A poetaster
 has framed the following {#samasyApUraRa#} stanza with the rule {#upasargAH#}
 {#kriyAyoge#} (<ls>Pāṇini I. 4. 59</ls>): {#upasargAH kriyAyoge pARinerapi saMmatam .#}
 {#nizkiyo'pi tavArAtiH sopasargaH sadA kaTam ..#} {%Upasargas%} are 20
 in number:-- {#pra, parA, apa, sam, anu, ava, nis#} or {#nir, dus#}
 or {#dur, vi, A(N), ni, aDi, api, ati, su, ut, aBi, prati, pari#},
 {#upa#}; or 22 if {#nis-nir#} and {#dus-dur#} be taken as separate words.
 There are two theories as to the character of these prepositions. According
 to one theory roots have various meanings in themselves ({#anekArTA hi#}
 {#DAtavaH#}); when prepositions are prefixed to them they simply bring to
 light those meanings already existent but hidden in them, but they do
 not {%express%} them, being meaningless themselves; <ab>cf.</ab> <ls>Śi. 10. 15</ls>:-- {#santameva#}
 {#ciramaprakftatvAdaprakASitamadidyutadaNge . viBramaM maDumadaH pramadAnAM#}
 {#DAtulInamupasarga ivArTam ..#} According to the other theory prepositions
 {%express%} their own independent meanings; they modify, intensify, and
 sometimes entirely alter, the senses of roots; <ab>cf.</ab> <ls>Sk.</ls>:-- {#upasargeRa#}
 {#DAtvarTo balAdanyatra nIyate . prahArAhArasaMhAravihAraparihAravat ..#}
 <ab>cf.</ab> also {#DAtvarTaM bADate kaScitkaScittamanuvartate . tameva viSinazwapanya#}
 {#upasargagatistriDA ..#} (The latter theory appears to be more correct.
 For a fuller exposition see Nirukta).
